                lcs_lengths[i][j] = lcs_lengths[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                lcs_lengths[i][j] = max(lcs_lengths[i - 1][j], lcs_lengths[i][j - 1])

    # Find the LCS using the LCS lengths
    lcs = ""
    i, j = m, n
    while I > 0 and j > 0:
        if str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1]:
            lcs = str1[i - 1] + lcs
            I -= 1
            j -= 1
        elif lcs_lengths[i - 1][j] > lcs_lengths[i][j - 1]:
            I -= 1
        else:
            j -= 1

    return lcs
```

This function takes two input strings `str1` and `str2` as arguments and returns their longest common subsequence as a string. It uses dynamic programming to build a 2D array `lcs_lengths` that stores the length of the longest common subsequence ending at each position `(i, j)` in `str1` and `str2`, respectively.

To reconstruct the actual LCS, we traverse the `lcs_lengths` array from the bottom-right corner to the top-left corner, appending characters to the `lcs` string as we go along.

